 "introductionText": "What FRUS answers directly: across the 553 volumes indexed, about one document in a hundred uses the language of corruption, bribery, graft or embezzlement, and the printed record shows corruption entering U.S. policy in three ways: as a measure of whether an aid recipient could survive (Nationalist China 1944-48, Korea and the Philippines 1961), as a lever for U.S. pressure on a dependent government (Cuba 1922), and, from 1974, as a diplomatic liability created by American firms' payments abroad (Saudi Arabia, Honduras, Western Europe), which ended in the Foreign Corrupt Practices Act and the 1980 debate over its export cost. Where FRUS points for the rest: the documents came chiefly from the Department of State's central files (Record Group 59: the Central Decimal File, then the 1964-73 central files and the Central Foreign Policy File), from CIA files, and from the presidential libraries' National Security Files (Kennedy, naId 1164; Johnson, naId 567979; Nixon, naId 572235); and from RG 59 lots such as the Marshall Mission Files (Lot 54D270) and the NSC policy papers (Lot 63D351, naId 2839192, entry A1 1586E, 1947-1979, partly restricted). The editors' footnotes point, without printing, to the same central-file classes and to RG 59 Lots 66D95 and 63D351 (naId 2839192), 62D1 (Policy Planning Council, naId 2838992, 1948-1961) and 62D430 (naId 2839191, 1953-1961). What this request cannot get from FRUS: nothing after the series ends (no 1990s document matched); the withheld names in the 1970s reporting (about one matching document in six from 1969 on carries a 'not declassified' bracket); and enclosures the editors did not print, such as Crowder's 1922 memorandum on graft in Cuba, which stays in the Central Decimal File.",
